comment.narrative:
1.1. - Two Regional Meetings of GEMs and regular follow-up and facilitation of knowledge sharing;
- Up to 4 small scale projects supported.
1. 2. - Four workshops at national level for improved data collection and development of SOPs on quantitative and qualitative gender-responsive career monitoring;
- Regional workshop to review methodology for regional data collection and development of SOPs;
- Data collected, and analysis conducted for the regional study. 
1. 3. - 1 Regional workshop;
- Knowledge tool development developed;
- Implementation of 2 recommendations from knowledge tool in each country.
2.1 - One regional meeting of Gender Trainers;
- At least three national briefings held in each country; 
- Regional ToT implemented by Regional Network of Gender Trainers;
2.2 - Two gender awareness workshops for teaching staff;
- Review of curricula initiated;
- 1 commanding officer seminar;
- 1 Gender Coach Programme initiated;
- 1 Regional meeting.

1. - A Regional Meeting of GEMs in the MoDs and Armed Forces (AFs) was organized online on 23-24 September 2020;
- Five small-scale projects supporting evidence-based and gender responsive human resources (HR) policies, as well as to advancement of MODs capacities in addressing gender-based discrimination were supported in North Macedonia (one project), Serbia (two projects) and Montenegro (two projects).
2. - The meeting of the Regional Working Group for development of the 2nd Regional Study on The Position of Women in the AFs in the Western Balkans was organized in February 2020. On this occasion, the Working Group members agreed on the methodology for the Regional Study and the initiation of the study; 
- MoDs in BiH, Montenegro and North Macedonia collected data on the agreed methodology and submitted it to UNDP SEESAC. 
3. - A Regional Working Group meeting held online on 22 May 2020;
- A final draft of the regional Manual on Combating Gender Based Discrimination, Sexual Harassment and Abuse was developed. The completion of the Manual was delayed in 2020 due to the ongoing pandemics. The Manual will be finalized and sent to the Working Group for final comments in Q1 2021, before submitting it to MODs for approval.
2.1 - 5th Regional Meeting of Military Gender Trainers organized online, on 21 October 2020;
- One national briefing was delivered by two gender military trainers in North Macedonia.
2.2 - Review of curricula was initiated by drafting the Concept note with defining entry points for gender mainstreaming in the military curricula and the translation of the seminal DCAF’s handbook Teaching Gender in the Military. Both, the concept paper and translation were intended to ensure a shared understanding among MoDs of the issue at hand and facilitate joint planning of the follow up activities;
- One Gender Coach Programme was completed in MoD of BiH, and two Gender Coach sessions were conducted in MoD of Montenegro.

1.1. •	Two (2) Regional Meetings of GEMs and regular follow-up and facilitation of knowledge sharing;
•	Up to 4 small scale projects supported. 
1.2 •	Four (4) workshops at national level for improved data collection and development related to gender-responsive human resources polices 
•	Regional workshop to review methodology for regional data collection and development of SOPs;
•	Data collected, and analysis conducted for the regional study.
1.3 •	1 Regional workshop related to combating gender-based discrimination;
•	Knowledge tool developed;
•	Implementation of 2 recommendations from knowledge tool in each country.
2.1 •	One regional meeting of Gender Trainers;
•	At least three national briefings held in each country;
•	Regional Gender ToT implemented by Regional Network of Gender Trainers;
2.2 •	Two gender awareness workshops held for teaching staff;
•	Activities related to integration of Gender into Military Education initiated;
•	1 commanding officer seminar; 
•	1 Gender coach program initiated;
•	1 Regional meeting related to integration of Gender into Military Education

Low percentage of women in the AFs in the Western Balkans, particularly in the command positions.
Over 4,700 offices, NCOs and soldiers trained/briefed on gender equality which is an insufficient number considering the size of the Armed Forces; 
Gender training  not institutionalized as part of professional development training;
Limited knowledge of commanding officers on gender in the military; 
Limited knowledge on how to mainstream gender in military education.

Nine (9) Regional Meetings of Gender Equality Mechanism (GEMs) and three (3) Regional Meetings of Gender Trainers held in Phase I (from 2012 to 2016).;

Low level of  MoD and AFs capacities for evidence based and gender responsive policy development; 

Limited capacities of MoDs and AFs for combating gender based discrimination, sexual harassment and abuse.
